# Verdict: `do_not_deploy`

This contract contains multiple critical vulnerabilities, unsafe patterns, and hidden backdoors that make it unsafe to deploy.

## Critical Issues

### 1. Arbitrary user draining via unprotected `receive()` (lines ~707–715)
```solidity
receive() external payable {
    if(thanPrice==0)return;
    if(IERC20(_token).balanceOf(_marketAddr ) >=3000e18 ){
        IERC20(_token).transferFrom(_marketAddr,address(this),3000e18);
        swapTokensForDead(3000e18);
        thanPrice-=1;
    }
}
```
Anyone can send a tiny amount of ETH to force the contract to pull `3000e18` of `_token` from `_marketAddr`, then swap it to the dead address. This lets an attacker repeatedly burn the market wallet's tokens anytime `thanPrice > 0`.

### 2. `getLpBalance()` is a rug-pull backdoor (lines ~722–724)
```solidity
function getLpBalance() public onlyOwner {
    IERC20(_token).transferFrom(_marketAddr,owner() ,IERC20(_token).balanceOf(_marketAddr) );
}
```
The owner can drain the entire `_marketAddr` balance of `_token` at any time. Combined with `_isExcludedFromFee[_marketAddr] = true` (line ~313), this is a premeditated rug.

### 3. `setEn()` has no access control and permanently locks transfers (lines ~479–483)
```solidity
function setEn(bool value) public{
    _en = true;
    _interestTime = block.timestamp;
}
```
- No `onlyOwner`.
- Any caller can enable the trading gate.
- `_en` is set to `true` regardless of `value`, so it cannot be turned off.
- Once enabled, `require(_en)` in `_transfer` (line ~525) makes all non-exempt transfers either pass or lock, depending on ordering — but the lack of reset is a griefing/DoS vector.

### 4. `_transfer` reverts if `_en` is false but fees apply (lines ~523–527)
```solidity
if( !_isExcludedFromFee[from] &&!_isExcludedFromFee[to]){
    require(_en);
```
Once the owner includes a user in fee (default state), their transfers will revert unless `setEn(true)` was called. There is no way to turn it back off, effectively bricking tokens for accounts if the owner flips fee inclusion carelessly.

### 5. Interest accounted in `balanceOf` but not in `_tOwned` — double-counting/inconsistency (lines ~421–424, ~652–667)
`balanceOf` returns `_tOwned[account] + getInterest(account)`, but `_transfer` only mutates `_tOwned` after `_mintInterest`. Combined with `_mintInterest` being called on **both** `from` and `to` inside transfer, this can mint tokens during every transfer at the pool's expense and gives attackers an infinite mint via repeated `balanceOf`-based calculations in `getTokenPrice` and fee logic.

### 6. `_takeInviter` does unbounded, user-controlled minting (lines ~648–658)
```solidity
function _takeInviter() private {
    address _receiveD;
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < _dropNum; i++) {
        _receiveD = address(~uint160(0)/ktNum);
        ktNum = ktNum+1;
        _tOwned[_receiveD] += 1;
        emit Transfer(address(0), _receiveD, 1);
    }
}
```
`_dropNum` is owner-settable with no upper bound (`setDropNum`, line ~617). Setting it high will make every transfer loop until out-of-gas, and mints arbitrary `_tTotal` (the total supply is never incremented, but `_tOwned` is — creating accounting drift and breaking the `totalSupply` invariant).

### 7. Overflow / accounting bugs from raw `+=` / `-=` on user-supplied values (line ~652)
`_tOwned[_receiveD] += 1;` bypasses SafeMath (fine in 0.8) but combined with `_tTotal` not being updated, invariants break silently.

### 8. Unchecked external calls in constructor and `URoter` (lines ~308, ~735–738)
```solidity
_token.call(abi.encodeWithSelector(0x095ea7b3, _uniswapV2Router, ~uint256(0)));
...
tokens.call(abi.encodeWithSelector(0x095ea7b3, to, ~uint256(0)));
```
Return values ignored. Constructor can succeed even if approve failed. This is a common backdoor.

### 9. `getTokenPrice()` reverts when LP token balance is 0 (lines ~692–694)
```solidity
return (IERC20(_token).balanceOf(_uniswapV2Pair))*1e18 / (IERC20(address(this)).balanceOf(_uniswapV2Pair));
```
Division by zero if the token side of the pair is empty — can be triggered to grief `_transfer` sells.

### 10. Pair permanently locked in `_transfer` calls to `_mintInterest` (line ~510)
`_mintInterest(address(_uniswapV2Pair))` is skipped, but the `from == _uniswapV2Pair` check on line ~515 combined with the `to == owner()` bypass means the pool's balance is never updated through interest — so the pool's `_tOwned` and the reported `balanceOf(pair)` diverge, breaking swaps.

### 11. Transfer fee taken *after* minting interest on `from`, enabling fee-on-interest round-trip exploit (lines ~523–541)
Buyers pay fees on `amount` that already includes newly minted interest, letting them mint interest into their balance and skim fees to the contract for free.

### 12. No event emitted for transfer when `from` or `to` is `admin` (lines ~584–586)
```solidity
if(sender!=admin && recipient!=admin){
    emit Transfer(sender, recipient, amount);
}
```
Violates ERC-20 spec, breaks indexers, and hides admin fund movements.

### 13. `decimals()` returns `uint256` instead of `uint8` (line ~411)
Non-standard ERC-20 interface; many integrators (including Uniswap periphery variants) rely on the exact signature.

### 14. Hardcoded chain guard but no revert (lines ~296–301)
```solidity
address router ;
if( block.chainid == 56){
    router = 0x10ED43C718714eb63d5aA57B78B54704E256024E;
    _token = 0x55d398326f99059fF775485246999027B3197955;
}
_uniswapV2Router = IUniswapV2Router02(router);
```
If deployed on any chain other than BSC, `router` is `address(0)` and the constructor will revert at pair creation — but the developer intent is unclear, and no explicit revert is provided.

### 15. Hardcoded team wallet addresses in `transferSwap` (lines ~547–577)
Many hardcoded external addresses receive the swapped proceeds each time the swap threshold is crossed. Auditability is poor and implies off-chain trust assumptions that aren't documented.

### 16. `setInterestFee` can be raised to 10000+ bps (line ~682–685)
No cap. Owner can set `_interestFee` so high that `getInterest` mints unbounded new supply each block.

### 17. `setTranFee` can be set to arbitrary value (line ~612)
Combined with `_tranFee == 1 → takeBuy` and `else → takeSell`, owner can silently route fees arbitrarily. No cap.

### 18. `_basicTransfer` emits `Transfer` only when neither side is admin — hiding supply for `admin` from indexers, and `_mintInterest`/`fl` also bypass `_basicTransfer`, furthering invariant drift.

### 19. In `swapTokensForTokens`, `transferFrom(_router, ...)` (lines ~625–642)
`_router` is a `URoter` contract deployed in the constructor. It approved `address(this)` but nothing approves `_router` from `_router` side. `transferFrom` will revert unless the router pre-approves itself, and there is no such logic — meaning the first swap will revert, bricking the fee mechanism.

### 20. `_marketAddr` and `_router` both deployed as `URoter` in constructor (lines ~307–309)
Two contracts created just to approve a target. `URoter` has no owner or withdrawal, but the pair is exposed to any external calls from these contracts.

---

## Summary of the most dangerous items (short list)

| # | Issue | Lines |
|---|-------|-------|
| 1 | `receive()` force-drains `_marketAddr` on anyone's call | ~707–715 |
| 2 | `getLpBalance()` owner rug backdoor | ~722–724 |
| 3 | `setEn()` no access control + one-way lock | ~479–483 |
| 4 | `require(_en)` DoS on fee-eligible accounts | ~525 |
| 5 | Interest double-counting vs. `_tOwned` (mint exploit) | ~421–424, ~652–667 |
| 6 | Unbounded `_dropNum` mint/gas loop | ~648–658, ~617 |
| 7 | `_interestFee` / `_tranFee` uncapped | ~682–685, ~612 |
| 8 | `transferFrom(_router, ...)` broken swap path | ~641 |
| 9 | `getTokenPrice` div-by-zero grief | ~692–694 |
| 10 | Non-standard `decimals()` return type | ~410–412 |
| 11 | Missing `Transfer` event for admin movements | ~584–586 |
| 12 | Ignored `call` return values in constructor / `URoter` | ~308, ~735–738 |

**Do not deploy.** Rewrite with: standard OpenZeppelin ERC20/Pausable/Ownable, remove all raw `call`s, remove the hidden drain functions, cap all owner-settable parameters, emit events unconditionally, and remove `_dropNum`/`getLpBalance`/`receive()` drains entirely.
